{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1515736-necrobotany","id":1515736,"title":"Necrobotany","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-06-21T04:04:35-07:00","id":1515736,"title":"Necrobotany","content":"I really like the idea of vampires keeping undead gardens. It makes for a good set piece for NPCs, but I could also see some PC concepts that would make use of it. I decided to gather some of the mentions in canon material I have access to (V20 and previous) and create an overview.<br /> Also, for the sake of simplicity, I will include Necromycology in this under the same name.<br /> <br /> We will start with that one; some <b>Nosferatu</b> have rather famously cultivated fungal gardens with strange properties. It isn\u2019t stated that vitae is involved, but it would be a good guess. A mentioned property of the results is a strong hallucinogenic effect.<br /> <br /> The <b>Lasombra</b> Caridad de Flores from <i>Mexico City by Night</i> maintains a private blood garden, and is able to use it to create poison that acts on kindred, <i>kalif</i>, but also entirely aesthetic effects. She is proficient in <b>Abyss Mysticism</b>, but it is unclear whether that factors into her gardening.<br /> <br /> Speaking of <i>kalif</i>, of course the <b>Banu Haqim</b> know about growing weed with vitae, too. It\u2019s probable that there is more <b>Dur-An-Ki </b>alchemy happening like that.<br /> <br /> Disappointingly, I could find very little about <b>Lhiannan</b> horticulture. <b>Ogham</b> can animate plants with vitae, but is more focused on Names later on.<br /> <br /> Next, there are the <b>Lhaka</b> (Bahari vampires), who cultivate allegorical as well as real gardens as part of their devotion to Lilith. The <i>Guide to the Tal\u2019mahe\u2019ra</i> gives us the Nightshade <b>Necromancy</b> Path, for fruit that gives Deathsight and the creation of plant zombies.<br /> The same book also has a <b>Vicissitude</b> power that, by pouring vitae into soil, warps plants and enables fruit to ghoul those who eat it. Enoch has the Gardens of Malkav, in which the <b>Rafastio</b> experiment with ghouling plants. The book says they only had success with carnivorous plants, which is mighty silly (except for trapping mechanisms they are no different from other plants), so I\u2019m reading it as fungi, which are at least decomposers.<br /> <br /> And taking a peek outside, V:tR has ghouled plants as <i>Mandragora</i>, which can produce a substance called Lacrima. It\u2019s basically a drug for vampires.<br /> <br /> <br /> From the above examples, a few commonalities can be gleamed:<br /> <br /> - Plants need to be fed with vitae. Usually, small amounts are sufficient, but doing it right is a learned ability. Just pouring some vitae on the soil does not work. Sometimes, the soil is enriched with corpses, or even the ashes of a slain vampire.<br /> <br /> - Botanical drugs or poisons can be enhanced in potency to a point where they can affect vampires.<br /> <br /> - Some mystical properties of vitae (bonding, ghouling, light Discipline effects) can be propagated through fruit of the plants. Commonly, the ratio invested to effectiveness is increased that way.<br /> <br /> - Plants change their morphology to reflect the vitae donor\u2019s Disciplines. Potence makes them huge, Obtenebration gloomy. Would be interesting to see a garden with one of the more exotic Disciplines.<br /> <br /> For interested PCs, I would realize this through a new Knowledge Ability (<b>Blood Gardening</b>), rather than a dedicated power. It\u2019s not exactly blood sorcery, rather limited in scope, and seems to be practiced throughout all kinds of Clans and Bloodlines.<br /> <br /> <br /> Have you used Blood Gardens in your chronicle? I\u2019m curious to see what others have done with the idea.<br /> <br /> Also, if I missed out on important examples, let me know.<br /> \u200b","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-21T10:56:34-07:00","id":1515762,"title":"","content":"'Undead' gardens sends really the wrong message. A dead plant can't be more valuable than a live plant, and it's quite the masquerade breach. There are going to be special vampire gardens, but I think you're being too optimistic with the extent. The canabis used for Kalif isn't going to come across as anything more than an impressive strain to most experts, it won't shatter the masquerade, but an undead plant would cause some problems. Plus Magic in the world of Darkness isn't terribly abundant and any kind of magic garden is going to need magic resources or be placed somewhere like a node and those tend to get contested. I'm sure some factions might be willing to pay the price but it won't be common. <br /> <br /> Abyss mysticism certainly won't be good for plants, the abyss is hostile to all life, but I think I did read somewhere that the bahari believe that Tzmisce and Lasombra got their signature powers by raiding lilith's garden, so maybe there's something there. Whatever it is, it's an abomination that should magnetize hunters to it.<br /> <br /> The further a species is away from human, the less magical and the more physical the alterations will get. Humans don't physically change at all, Horses or dogs on Vitae will look like they're on Trenbolone, rats will double their mass while fingernail-sized spiders will grow a hundred fold o palm-sized. Animals strictly have discipline limitations and can never learn things like Obtenebration (or protean, which I think is stupid, because it was made for them, but anyhow) With that in mind, I think plants will absorb vitae with extreme inefficiency and the donor's disciplines won't give them anything special. <br /> <br /> -Assamites are all about poisons so they often have gardens, mostly with ordinary poisonous plants. It's not uncommon for them to be harvesting snakes or insects. Most assamites will prefer more easily managable sources of poison though, like those found in home cleaning or industrial uses <br /> -Bahari have gardens for religious reasons. And because they're witches and witches have strong associations with poisons. Did you know many women accused of witchcraft were actually guilty? Poisonings were considered witchcraft. (poisoning an abusive husband you can't deal with might be a cause deserving sympathy, but it's still premeditated murder) <br /> -Followers and Ravnos will no doubt be interested in more recreational options. As might many cults. Will they augment the plants? In some cases. Setites will augment the drugs they make to be sure. <br /> -Lhiannan are going to want defensive plants: blackberry bushes, roses, anything nasty; I think willows might be good due to the amount of limbs they have. they'd probably go for Cacti if they ever made it. This goes for other green path users. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-21T11:32:27-07:00","id":1515765,"title":"","content":"During one Tremere centric game we used the Green Path to build living focal points on top of ley lines and such. The trees and plants were both laid out in arcane patterns and were chosen for their occult meaning. The plants were then treated with alchemical catalysts to promote magical conductivity. Copious amounts of vitae were also poured over the plants to ensure their proper attunement to thaumaturgical processes. It's not a blood garden per se, but it allowed for some nice house rule bonuses for casting rituals and what not.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_li